    <!--
            <question id="arch-usecases" when="init">
                <hint>
                    Content of this answer will be displayed as part of page at
                    http://www.netbeans.org/download/dev/javadoc/usecases.html
                    You can use tags &lt;usecase name="name&gt; regular html description &lt;/usecase&gt;
                    and if you want to use an URL you can prefix if with @TOP@ to begin
                    at the root of your javadoc
                </hint>

                Describe the main <a href="https://netbeans.apache.org/wiki/API_Design#The_Importance_of_Being_Use_Case_Oriented">
                use cases</a> of the new API. Who will use it under
                what circumstances? What kind of code would typically need to be written
                to use the module?
            </question>
    -->
    <answer id="arch-usecases">
        <p>
            A generic project is a plain directory the user wants to manage as a
            project (no build system, no specific language): a collection of documents,
            scripts or data files with a folder view and per-project settings.
        </p>
        <usecase id="open-folder" name="Open a folder as generic project">
            <p>
                A user right-clicks a non-project folder (e.g. in Favorites or in a
                project view) and chooses "Open as Generic Project", or creates one via
                the New Project wizard. The folder gets a generic project node with a
                folder tree and is opened in the project tabs.
            </p>
        </usecase>
        <usecase id="share-project" name="Share a generic project with a team">
            <p>
                With the marker file enabled (the default), the shared settings
                (display name, file filter pattern) live in the <code>.nbgp</code> marker
                file in the working tree and are versioned along with the project; team
                members checking out the folder get the project and its settings.
            </p>
        </usecase>
        <usecase id="local-project" name="Work on a folder without writing into it">
            <p>
                The marker file can be disabled in the project customizer; the project
                then exists via the FileObject attribute only and all settings (shared
                and private) are stored in the user configuration, so nothing is written
                into the folder.
            </p>
        </usecase>
    </answer>



